The two Pennsylvania points I checked are now confirmed in primary sources, though the Act 146 details differ from what I wrote earlier. Act 146 of 2022 (SB 225, signed November 3, 2022) amends the Insurance Company Law of 1921, and it is **effective immediately with exceptions**. It is a 2022 act, not 2024. The act also gives covered persons a right to request **external review of whether the insurer complied with the No Surprises Act's surprise-billing and cost-sharing protections**, and that request goes to the Pennsylvania Insurance Department. <citation src="2,3"></citation>

## Act 146 and the PID route

The most useful takeaway for your case is that the insurer's own denial notice must tell you about this right. The statutory notice language says you may have the right to review of whether the plan complied with the No Surprises Act, and that you may submit an external review request to the Pennsylvania Insurance Department. <citation src="2"></citation> The PID's consumer contact, 1-877-881-6388, matches what the framework lists. <citation src="4"></citation>

Two caveats. Federal sources describe enforcement as shared: most states partner with the federal government, and the federal government enforces for self-funded plans that state law does not reach. <citation src="1"></citation> So the fully insured versus self-funded question still decides which regulator takes the complaint. I also could not confirm the CMS number 1-800-985-3059 from these results. The consumer sources I found list **1-800-MEDICARE (1-800-633-4227)** for No Surprises Act questions, so please check the number on cms.gov/nosurprises before you use it in a letter. <citation src="5"></citation>

## The six-year period

I confirmed the general six-year catch-all in **42 Pa.C.S. § 5527(b)**, which applies to civil actions not covered by another specific limitation period. <citation src="7,10"></citation> Pennsylvania courts have applied that catch-all to statutory damages claims outside the contract context. <citation src="8"></citation> That supports treating six years as the likely period for a UTPCPL claim, but I did not find a decision specifically on UTPCPL, so the framework should still say "generally applied" rather than state it as settled.

## Corrections to the contract period

The four-year period for written contracts is in the § 5525 series. A 2001 law review article describes it as § 5525(8) under the 1982 scheme, and the current code appears to number it § 5525(a)(8). <citation src="9"></citation> The framework cites § 5525(a) generally, so the subsection should be made specific. I also have not verified that § 5533(b) is the right provision for how partial payments or acknowledgments restart the clock. That needs a primary-source check before the framework relies on it.

## What remains open

I did not resolve the FCEUA subsection pin cites, the 501(r) timing rules in 26 C.F.R. § 1.501(r)-6, or the judgment and exemption rules. Those stay flagged. Your tracker's October 2024 dates may still be template placeholders, as I noted before. Do you know yet whether the plan is fully insured or self-funded? That answer decides whether the PID or CMS complaint narrative should go first.



