7. Dispute Letter Template
[Your Full Name]
[Street Address]
[City, PA ZIP]
[Phone] | [Email]

[Date]

VIA CERTIFIED MAIL, RETURN RECEIPT REQUESTED
Tracking #: [Number]

[Provider Name]
Attn: Billing Department
[Provider Street Address]
[City, State ZIP]

Re: Dispute of Balance Bill, Account # [Account Number]
    Date(s) of Service: [Dates]
    Amount Disputed: $[Amount]

Dear Billing Manager:

I am writing to dispute the balance bill referenced above. I am disputing this amount in writing and request that you respond in writing.

1. Basis for dispute

[Select the applicable statement and delete the others.]

[Option A, emergency services:] The services were emergency services under the No Surprises Act, 42 U.S.C. § 300gg-111 and Public Health Service Act §§ 2799A-1 through 2799A-3, as implemented at 45 C.F.R. Part 149. Balance billing for emergency services is limited to in-network cost-sharing.

[Option B, ancillary services at an in-network facility:] The services were non-emergency services by an out-of-network ancillary provider (for example, anesthesiology, radiology, or pathology) at an in-network facility. Under 45 C.F.R. Part 149, such providers may not balance bill beyond in-network cost-sharing. A consent waiver does not apply to ancillary services.

[Option C, other non-emergency services:] The services were non-emergency services by an out-of-network provider at an in-network facility. I did not sign a valid written notice and consent waiver provided at least 72 hours before the service. [Describe the situation.]

I am not responsible for any amount above my in-network cost-sharing obligation. Under the framework established by 45 C.F.R. Part 149, the health plan and provider are responsible for resolving payment disputes through open negotiation and, if needed, Independent Dispute Resolution.

2. Pennsylvania law

To the extent you are a creditor collecting this balance, the Fair Credit Extension Uniformity Act, 73 P.S. § 2270.1 et seq., applies to your collection conduct. Collecting or attempting to collect an amount not owed may violate 73 P.S. § 2270.4 **[VERIFY subsection pin cite]**. A violation of that Act may also violate the Unfair Trade Practices and Consumer Protection Law, 73 P.S. § 201-1 et seq.

3. Requests

Please:

a. Stop all collection activity on this account, including calls, statements, and referrals to any collection agency, while this dispute is pending.
b. Correct the account to reflect only my in-network cost-sharing obligation, or provide a written explanation of the basis for the balance.
c. Confirm in writing that the account will not be reported to any credit reporting agency as past due or in collection while this dispute is pending.
d. [If applicable:] Provide your Financial Assistance Policy, a plain-language summary, and a FAP application.

Please respond in writing within 30 days. This is my request for a response. It is not a legal deadline, but I will consider the account disputed until I receive a written answer.

I will file complaints with the appropriate regulator if this matter is not resolved:
- Pennsylvania Insurance Department, Consumer Services Bureau: 1-877-881-6388 (fully insured plans)
- CMS / federal No Surprises Act channel: confirm the current number and complaint portal at cms.gov/nosurprises before sending (1-800-985-3059 was not independently verified; 1-800-MEDICARE, 1-800-633-4227, is listed in consumer sources for No Surprises Act questions)

Enclosures:
- Copy of balance bill (highlighted)
- Explanation of Benefits
- Documentation of service dates and provider type
- [If applicable:] Written notice and consent form, if any

Sincerely,

[Signature]
[Printed Name]

